{%- comment -%}
  The inventory rule for the whole theme, in one place.

  "How many of this piece are left, and what do we say about it" was written three times:
  in card-product, in main-product and in lena-featured-piece. All three computed the same
  quantity the same way and then printed the same two sentences, differing only in the CSS
  class on the wrapper. They agreed by accident, not by construction - and one of them had
  already drifted.

  THE BUG THIS REPLACES (survey B2). In card-product the quantity was assigned at line 109,
  inside {%- if card_product.featured_media -%}, but read again at line 256, outside it. For
  a product with no featured image the variable was never set, so the "Only piece in
  existence" line silently disappeared - along with the badge, the New badge and the sold
  overlay, which all sat in the same branch. That is the identical fault that was found and
  fixed on the PDP as R19; card-product never got the same treatment.

  Hoisting the assign would have fixed it a third time in a third place. Moving the rule
  here fixes it once and makes it structurally impossible to reintroduce, because there is
  now no quantity variable at any call site to read out of scope.

  USAGE

    {%- render 'lena-stock', product: card_product, style: 'card', part: 'badge' -%}
    {%- render 'lena-stock', product: card_product, style: 'card', part: 'scarcity' -%}

    product  the product to read. Required; renders nothing if absent.
    style    'card' | 'pdp' | 'featured' - picks the CSS class only, never the wording.
             Anything else falls back to 'card'.
    part     'badge'    -> the "1 of 1" / "N in stock" pill
             'scarcity' -> the "Only piece in existence" line
             Two parts rather than one block because the call sites put them in different
             places: the badge sits over the image, the line sits under the price.

  ONE DELIBERATE BEHAVIOUR CHANGE. The scarcity line is gated on `product.available` as
  well as a quantity of 1. card-product and main-product both already did this;
  lena-featured-piece tested the quantity alone. That was safe there only because its pool
  is pre-filtered with `where: 'available'`, so the extra test changes nothing today and
  stops the section depending on a filter applied somewhere else.

  WHY NOT ALSO THE SOLD OVERLAY. "This piece found its home" is also written twice, but the
  two are genuinely different elements - card-product paints it across the image as an
  overlay, main-product prints it as a line under the price. Only the sentence is shared,
  and sharing a sentence is not worth a snippet that has to render two unrelated shapes.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign ls_qty = product.selected_or_first_available_variant.inventory_quantity | default: 0

  case style
    when 'pdp'
      assign ls_badge_class = 'lena-pdp-badge-1of1'
      assign ls_scarcity_class = 'lena-pdp-scarcity'
    when 'featured'
      assign ls_badge_class = 'lena-fp-badge'
      assign ls_scarcity_class = 'lena-fp-scarcity'
    else
      assign ls_badge_class = 'lena-badge-1of1'
      assign ls_scarcity_class = 'lena-scarcity'
  endcase
-%}

{%- if part == 'scarcity' -%}
  {%- if product.available and ls_qty == 1 -%}
    <div class="{{ ls_scarcity_class }}">
      <span class="dm filled sm"></span> Only piece in existence
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- if ls_qty > 0 -%}
    <div class="{{ ls_badge_class }}">
      <span class="dm gold sm"></span>
      {%- if ls_qty == 1 -%}
        1 of 1
      {%- else -%}
        {{ ls_qty }} in stock
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
